@rem Exercises for lesson 01_basics (C++ track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise cpp-basics-theory-header
@type theory
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which header do you include to use cout and cin?
@answer
<iostream> | iostream | #include <iostream>
@explain
cout and cin are declared in <iostream>.

@exercise cpp-basics-choice-cin
@type choice
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which statement reads an integer from the keyboard into the variable age?
@choices
* cin >> age;
- cin << age;
- cin >> &age;
- cout >> age;
@explain
cin >> variable reads into the variable itself; unlike scanf it needs no & and no format string.

@exercise cpp-basics-predict-bool
@type predict
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
cout << (3 > 2) << " " << (3 < 2) << endl;
@answer
1 0
@explain
cout prints a true comparison as 1 and a false one as 0.

@exercise cpp-basics-predict-reference
@type predict
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void addOne(int &x) {
    x = x + 1;
}

int main() {
    int a = 5;
    addOne(a);
    addOne(a);
    cout << a << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
7
@explain
x is a reference to the caller's variable, so both calls change a: 5 + 1 + 1 = 7.

@exercise cpp-basics-predict-value-vs-ref
@type predict
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void f(int x) { x = 100; }
void g(int &x) { x = 200; }

int main() {
    int a = 1, b = 1;
    f(a);
    g(b);
    cout << a << " " << b << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
1 200
@explain
f works on a copy, so a stays 1. g receives a reference to b and changes it.

@exercise cpp-basics-fill-swap-ref
@type fill
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Make swapInts swap the caller's variables by taking references.
@code
void swapInts(int ___ a, int ___ b) {
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}
@answer
&
&
@explain
A & after the type makes the parameter a reference to the caller's variable.

@exercise cpp-basics-bug-by-value
@type bug
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 2
@prompt
This program should print 6, but it prints 5. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void addOne(int x) {
    x = x + 1;
}

int main() {
    int a = 5;
    addOne(a);
    cout << a << endl;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 4
@answer
void addOne(int &x) {
@explain
x is a copy of a. Taking the parameter by reference (int &x) lets the function change the caller's variable.

@exercise cpp-basics-theory-bool
@type theory
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which type holds only the values true and false?
@answer
bool | boolean | a bool
@explain
bool stores true or false; cout prints them as 1 and 0.

@exercise cpp-basics-theory-const
@type theory
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which keyword makes a variable unchangeable after its declaration?
@answer
const | constant
@explain
const int MAX = 100; cannot be assigned to again; the compiler reports an error if you try.

@exercise cpp-basics-choice-modulo
@type choice
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What is the value of 17 % 5?
@choices
- 3
* 2
- 3.4
- 12
@explain
% gives the remainder of an integer division: 17 = 3 * 5 + 2.

@exercise cpp-basics-choice-uninit
@type choice
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does a local variable hold right after  int x;  ?
@choices
- 0
- -1
* Nothing defined: it holds a leftover value and must not be read before it is assigned
- The address of x
@explain
Local variables are not initialised automatically. Reading one before assigning it gives an unpredictable value.

@exercise cpp-basics-fill-cin-chain
@type fill
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Read two integers with one cin statement and print their sum.
@code
int a, b;
cin ___ a ___ b;
cout << "Sum: " << a + b << endl;
@answer
>>
>>
@explain
cin uses the extraction operator >> and can chain several reads; cout uses << to send values out.

@exercise cpp-basics-fill-half
@type fill
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 2
@prompt
For n = 7 the variable half should become 3.5. Fill in the divisor so that the division keeps the fraction.
@code
int n = 7;
double half = n / ___;
cout << half << endl;
@answer
2.0 | 2. | 2.0f
@explain
int / int is integer division (7 / 2 is 3). A double operand such as 2.0 makes the division a double one: 3.5.

@exercise cpp-basics-bug-assign-compare
@type bug
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 2
@prompt
The program should print "no" because age is not 18, but it prints "yes". Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int age = 20;
    if (age = 18) {
        cout << "yes" << endl;
    } else {
        cout << "no" << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
@bugline 6
@answer
if (age == 18) {
@explain
A single = assigns 18 to age (and the value 18 counts as true). Comparing needs ==.

@exercise cpp-basics-bug-char-literal
@type bug
@lesson 01_basics
@difficulty 1
@prompt
This code does not compile. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    char grade = "A";
    cout << grade << endl;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 5
@answer
char grade = 'A';
@explain
Double quotes make a string literal (several characters plus '\0'). A single char needs single quotes: 'A'.
